data.content:
These are two distinct C# 15 breaking changes:

## 1. `&&` with `dynamic` operand (DynamicOps.cs)

C# 15 no longer defers the entire `&&`/`||` expression to the runtime when one operand is `dynamic`. It now tries to resolve `operator true`/`operator false` at **compile time** on the static type of the non-dynamic operand (`IFilter`). Since `IFilter` has no `operator &`, the compiler can't construct the short-circuit `&&` operation—even though at runtime the concrete type (`CompositeFilter`) has one.

**Fix:** Avoid mixing `dynamic` with short-circuit operators on the interface type. Either:

```csharp
// Option A: use explicit two-step logic
void Apply(IFilter left, dynamic right)
{
    if (IFilter.op_True(left))
    {
        IFilter combined = (IFilter)((dynamic)left & right);
    }
}

// Option B: cast to the concrete type if known
void Apply(CompositeFilter left, dynamic right)
{
    var combined = left && right;  // works — CompositeFilter has operator&
}
```

## 2. `ref readonly` lambda missing well-known type (RefHelper.cs)

C# 15 now emits `System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RequiresLocationAttribute` on `ref readonly` lambda return types. If your target framework doesn't include this type (older TFM or missing reference), you get **CS0518**.

**Fix:** Either target `net11.0` (which includes the attribute), or polyfill it:

```csharp
namespace System.Runtime.CompilerServices
{
    [AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Parameter, Inherited = false)]
    internal sealed class RequiresLocationAttribute : Attribute { }
}
```

This lets the compiler emit the required metadata even on older TFMs.
